\date{2026-10-06}
\title{Equivalences on figures}
\author{ulrikbuchholtz}
\taxon{Module}
\import{stt-macros}

\p{A crisp map #{f : X \to Y} is an \em{equivalence on #{S}-figures} when it
induces an equivalence #{\modflat{(S \to X)} \simeq \modflat{(S \to Y)}} on
crisp maps out of #{S} (\ref{ub-0032}), in Lawvere's terminology of figures of
a given shape \citek{Law07}. The main fact is that this condition glues along unions of
subtypes (\ref{ub-0033}), and hence along filtrations (\ref{ub-0034}): since a
union of subtypes is a pushout, mapping out of it is a pullback, and
#{\flat} preserves pullbacks. It is used to show that
[a map that is an equivalence on simplex-figures is one on ordered-subcube-figures](ub-003D).}

\agda{
module Modalities.Cohesion.Figures where

open import Axioms.UF
}

\agda-imports{
\agda{
open import Foundations.Prelude
open import Foundations.Spans

open import Modalities.Cohesion.Flat
open import Modalities.Cohesion.FlatLex
open import Modalities.Cohesion.LiftingSquares

open import Core.Arrows
open import Core.Joins
open import Core.SubtypeUnions
open import Core.CartesianSquares
open import Core.CocartesianSquares
open import Core.FunctorialityPullbacks
}
}

\subtree[ub-0032]{
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Equivalences on figures}
\date{2026-09-05}

\p{For a crisp type #{S}, a map #{S \to X} is a \em{figure of shape #{S}} in
#{X}, and #{\modflat{(S \to X)}} is the type of crisp #{S}-figures; in the
presheaf model it is the value #{X(S)} of #{X} at #{S}. A crisp
#{f : X \to Y} is an \em{equivalence on #{S}-figures} when
#{\modflat{(f \circ -)} : \modflat{(S \to X)} \to \modflat{(S \to Y)}} is an
equivalence, that is, when #{f \circ -} is a #{\flat}-equivalence. This is the
condition that a [separating family](stt-00J7) asks of a map on each of its
members.}

\p{The terminology of figures of a given shape is Lawvere's \citek{Law07}.
In abstract homotopy theory, a map #{f} for which #{\mathrm{Map}(S, f)} is an
equivalence is called an \em{#{S}-colocal equivalence}
\citet{Def. 3.1.8}{Hir03}, or an
\em{#{S}-cellular equivalence} \citek{Far96}; the notion here is the crisp
version, with #{\flat} of the mapping type in place of the mapping space.}

\p{Being an equivalence on #{S}-figures is a proposition, and it is invariant
under crisp equivalence of #{S}, since the two restriction maps commute with
the two comparisons on the nose. The invariance is used in two shapes: along a
bundled equivalence, and along a pair of fibrewise maps between crisp subtypes
of a crisp type, which is an equivalence as soon as it has a converse
(\ref{ub-0015}).}

\agda{
is-♭-equiv-on
  : ∀ {@♭ 𝓦 𝓤 𝓥} (@♭ S : Type 𝓦) {@♭ X : Type 𝓤} {@♭ Y : Type 𝓥}
  → (@♭ f : X → Y) → Type (𝓦 ⊔ 𝓤 ⊔ 𝓥)
is-♭-equiv-on S f = is-♭-equiv (postcomp S f)

module _ {@♭ 𝓤 𝓥} {@♭ X : Type 𝓤} {@♭ Y : Type 𝓥} (@♭ f : X → Y) where

  is-♭-equiv-on←equiv
    : ∀ {@♭ 𝓦 𝓦'} {@♭ S : Type 𝓦} {@♭ S' : Type 𝓦'}
      (@♭ e : S → S') → @♭ is-equiv e
    → is-♭-equiv-on S' f → is-♭-equiv-on S f
  is-♭-equiv-on←equiv {S = S} {S'} e ee h
    = 3-for-2' (is-♭-equiv←is-equiv (precomp-equiv {f = e} ee))
        (is-equiv←~ square
          (is-equiv-∘ (is-♭-equiv←is-equiv (precomp-equiv {f = e} ee)) h))
    where
      square
        : ♭-map (postcomp S f) ∘ ♭-map (precomp X e)
        ~ ♭-map (precomp Y e) ∘ ♭-map (postcomp S' f)
      square (mod♭ _) = refl

  is-♭-equiv-on←≃
    : ∀ {@♭ 𝓦 𝓦'} {@♭ S : Type 𝓦} {@♭ S' : Type 𝓦'}
      (@♭ e : S ≃ S') → is-♭-equiv-on S' f → is-♭-equiv-on S f
  is-♭-equiv-on←≃ e = is-♭-equiv-on←equiv (e ._≃_.fwd) (e ._≃_.fwd-is-equiv)

  is-♭-equiv-on←iff
    : ∀ {@♭ 𝓩 𝓦 𝓦'} {@♭ Z : Type 𝓩}
      {@♭ S : Z → Type 𝓦} {@♭ S' : Z → Type 𝓦'}
      (@♭ Sp : (z : Z) → is-prop (S z)) (@♭ S'p : (z : Z) → is-prop (S' z))
      (@♭ g : (z : Z) → S z → S' z) (@♭ g' : (z : Z) → S' z → S z)
    → is-♭-equiv-on (Σ Z S') f → is-♭-equiv-on (Σ Z S) f
  is-♭-equiv-on←iff Sp S'p g g' = is-♭-equiv-on←≃ (sub-incl≃ Sp S'p g g')
}
}

\subtree[ub-0033]{
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Equivalences on figures glue along unions}
\date{2026-09-05}

\p{\strong{Lemma.} Let #{P} and #{Q} be crisp families over a crisp type #{Z}.
If a crisp map #{f} is an equivalence on #{\sum_Z P}-, #{\sum_Z Q}- and
#{\sum_Z P \land Q}-figures, then it is one on #{\sum_Z P \lor Q}-figures.}

\p{This is the counterpart for mapping out of
[the glueing lemma](ub-000T) for anodyne maps, and it runs the other way
round. There the union was a pushout and a class of maps
closed under cobase change was glued along it. Here the union is again a
pushout (\ref{ub-0001}), so mapping out of it is a pullback (that is what
\code{up-cocartesian} says), and #{\flat} preserves pullbacks (\ref{stt-0058}).
So #{\modflat{(\sum_Z P \lor Q \to X)}} is the pullback of the top row of}

\quiver{
\begin{tikzcd}
	{\modflat{(\sum_Z P \to X)}} & {\modflat{(\sum_Z P \land Q \to X)}} & {\modflat{(\sum_Z Q \to X)}} \\
	{\modflat{(\sum_Z P \to Y)}} & {\modflat{(\sum_Z P \land Q \to Y)}} & {\modflat{(\sum_Z Q \to Y)}}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-2]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=1-2]
	\arrow[from=2-1, to=2-2]
	\arrow[from=2-3, to=2-2]
	\arrow["{f_*}", from=1-1, to=2-1]
	\arrow["{f_*}", from=1-2, to=2-2]
	\arrow["{f_*}", from=1-3, to=2-3]
\end{tikzcd}
}

\p{and #{\modflat{(\sum_Z P \lor Q \to Y)}} that of the bottom row. The three
vertical maps are equivalences by hypothesis, and
[a map of cospans that is an equivalence at each corner is an equivalence on
the pullbacks](stt-00BG), so #{f_*} is an equivalence on
#{\modflat{(\sum_Z P \lor Q \to -)}}, once it is checked that the map induced
on pullbacks is the one induced by #{f}.}

\p{That check is where the work is, and two naturality facts are all it
needs: #{\flat} of an
\code{ap} is the \code{ap} of a #{\flat} (\ref{stt-0109}), and function
extensionality is natural in the target (\ref{ub-0031}). Both are spent on
one thing, the third component of the gap map, which is where the pushout's
filler lives; the other two components agree on the nose, because
precomposition is associative.}

\agda{
♭-ap-funext
  : ∀ {@♭ 𝓒 𝓓 𝓤 𝓥} {@♭ C : Type 𝓒} {@♭ D : Type 𝓓}
      {@♭ X : Type 𝓤} {@♭ Y : Type 𝓥}
      (@♭ f : X → Y) (@♭ g : C → X) {@♭ u v : D → C} (@♭ H : u ~ v)
  → ap (♭-map (postcomp D f)) (ap♭ mod♭ (funext→ (g ◃h H)))
  ＝ ap♭ mod♭ (funext→ ((f ∘ g) ◃h H))
♭-ap-funext {D = D} f g H
  = ap-♭-map (postcomp D f) (funext→ (g ◃h H))
  ∙ ap♭-cong mod♭
      ( ap-postcomp-funext f (g ◃h H)
      ∙ ap funext→ (funext→ (λ c → sym (ap-∘ f g (H c)))))
}

\agda{
module ♭-equiv-on-union
  {@♭ 𝓤 𝓥} {@♭ X : Type 𝓤} {@♭ Y : Type 𝓥} (@♭ f : X → Y)
  {@♭ 𝓩 𝓟 𝓠} {@♭ Z : Type 𝓩} (@♭ P : Z → Type 𝓟) (@♭ Q : Z → Type 𝓠)
  where

  private
    AC = Arrow-map←Cocone (union-cocone P Q)
    SQ = transpose-square AC

    ♭SQ : ∀ {@♭ 𝓡} (@♭ R : Type 𝓡)
        → Arrow-map (♭-map (precomp R (AC .Arrow-map.bot)))
                    (♭-map (precomp R (AC .Arrow-map.top)))
    ♭SQ R = ♭Square (precomp-square SQ R)

    ♭gap : ∀ {@♭ 𝓡} (@♭ R : Type 𝓡)
         → is-equiv (gap-square (♭SQ R))
    ♭gap R
      = is-pullback←is-cartesian _
          (♭-is-cartesian (precomp-square SQ R) (union-is-cocartesian P Q R))

    CM : Cospan-map (Cone←Arrow-map (♭SQ X) .fst) (Cone←Arrow-map (♭SQ Y) .fst)
    CM .Cospan-map.h₁ = ♭-map (postcomp (Σ Z P) f)
    CM .Cospan-map.h₂ = ♭-map (postcomp (Σ Z (∩-fam P Q)) f)
    CM .Cospan-map.h₃ = ♭-map (postcomp (Σ Z Q) f)
    CM .Cospan-map.H (mod♭ _) = refl
    CM .Cospan-map.K (mod♭ _) = refl

  is-♭-equiv-on-∪
    : is-♭-equiv-on (Σ Z (∩-fam P Q)) f
    → is-♭-equiv-on (Σ Z P) f
    → is-♭-equiv-on (Σ Z Q) f
    → is-♭-equiv-on (Σ Z (∪-fam P Q)) f
  is-♭-equiv-on-∪ hC hP hQ
    = 3-for-2 (♭gap Y)
        (is-equiv←~ nat
          (is-equiv-∘ (Pullback₁-is-equiv CM (hP , hC , hQ)) (♭gap X)))
    where
      nat : gap-square (♭SQ Y) ∘ ♭-map (postcomp (Σ Z (∪-fam P Q)) f)
          ~ Pullback₁ CM ∘ gap-square (♭SQ X)
      nat (mod♭ g)
        = pullback-path→
            ( refl , refl
            , ∙-refll _ ∙ (∙-refll _ ∙ ap (_∙ refl) (♭-ap-funext f g _)) )
}
}

\subtree[ub-0034]{
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Equivalences on figures of a filtration}
\date{2026-09-05}

\p{The form the union lemma is used in. Let #{C_0, C_1, \ldots} be crisp
subtypes of #{Z}, and #{\bigvee_{l \le b} C_l} their
[increasing unions](ub-0046). A crisp map is an equivalence on
#{\sum_Z \bigvee_{l \le b} C_l}-figures as soon as it is one on the figures
of each cell #{C_l} and of each \em{incidence}, the part
#{(C_0 \lor \cdots \lor C_b) \land C_{b+1}} of the next cell already covered.
The incidences are supplied as subtypes #{D_{b+1}} together with a logical
equivalence, so that they can be named however is convenient. That is what
makes a filtration cheaper than a general finite union: the cells need not
be closed under meets, and only the incidences need to be named. For
[the staircases of a prism](ub-0024) each incidence is a single wall
(\ref{ub-002A}).}

\agda{
module ♭-equiv-on-filtration
  {@♭ 𝓤 𝓥} {@♭ X : Type 𝓤} {@♭ Y : Type 𝓥} (@♭ f : X → Y)
  {@♭ 𝓩 𝓒} {@♭ Z : Type 𝓩}
  (@♭ C : ℕ → Z → Type 𝓒) (@♭ D : ℕ → Z → Type 𝓒)
  (@♭ Cp : (l : ℕ) (z : Z) → is-prop (C l z))
  (@♭ Dp : (l : ℕ) (z : Z) → is-prop (D l z))
  where

  private
    ⋁C = ⋁fam C
    ⋁C-is-prop = ⋁fam-is-prop C Cp

  module _
    (@♭ inc→ : (b : ℕ) (z : Z) → ∩-fam (⋁C b) (C (suc b)) z → D (suc b) z)
    (@♭ inc← : (b : ℕ) (z : Z) → D (suc b) z → ∩-fam (⋁C b) (C (suc b)) z)
    (hC : (@♭ l : ℕ) → is-♭-equiv-on (Σ Z (C l)) f)
    (hD : (@♭ l : ℕ) → is-♭-equiv-on (Σ Z (D l)) f)
    where

    is-♭-equiv-on-⋁ : (@♭ b : ℕ) → is-♭-equiv-on (Σ Z (⋁fam C b)) f
    is-♭-equiv-on-⋁ zero = hC 0
    is-♭-equiv-on-⋁ (suc b)
      = ♭-equiv-on-union.is-♭-equiv-on-∪ f (⋁C b) (C (suc b))
          (is-♭-equiv-on←iff f (λ z → ×-is-prop (⋁C-is-prop b z) (Cp (suc b) z))
            (Dp (suc b)) (inc→ b) (inc← b) (hD (suc b)))
          (is-♭-equiv-on-⋁ b)
          (hC (suc b))
}
}
